## Test the refusal of "drbdmeta create-md" when internal meta data would take
## room a file system on the device still uses: it now says by how much, in both
## directions -- how much the file system has to give up, and how much bigger
## the device would have to be.
##
## Test layout: a 256 MB image with an ext2 super block written by hand -- 65536
## blocks of 4096 bytes, so the file system claims all 262144 kB of it.  With 31
## bitmap slots, 261860 kB are left over for data, 284 kB short.  Growing the
## device has to cover the bigger bitmap that comes with it, hence 288 kB.
##
## An ext2 super block starts at byte 1024: the block count at 0x404, the log2
## of the block size at 0x418, and the 0xEF53 magic at 0x438.

$ img=$(mktemp --suffix=.img) && tmpdir=$(mktemp -d)
$ truncate -s 268435456 "$img"
$ printf '\000\000\001\000' | dd of="$img" bs=1 seek=1028 count=4 conv=notrunc status=none
$ printf '\002' | dd of="$img" bs=1 seek=1048 count=1 conv=notrunc status=none
$ printf '\123\357' | dd of="$img" bs=1 seek=1080 count=2 conv=notrunc status=none
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal create-md 31 </dev/null 2>&1 | grep -v '^$' | tail -11 | sed -e "s|$img|IMG|"; echo "${PIPESTATUS[0]}"
      262144 kB data area apparently used
      261860 kB left usable by current configuration
Device size would be truncated, which
would corrupt data and result in
'access beyond end of device' errors.
You need to either
   * use external meta data (recommended)
   * shrink that filesystem by at least 284 kB first
   * grow IMG by at least 288 kB
   * zero out the device (destroy the filesystem)
Operation refused.
40
$ truncate -s $((268435456 + 290816)) "$img"
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal create-md 31 </dev/null 2>&1 | grep -v '^$' | tail -1; echo "${PIPESTATUS[0]}"
Operation refused.
40
$ truncate -s $((268435456 + 294912)) "$img"
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal create-md 31 </dev/null 2>&1 | grep -v '^$' | tail -1; echo "${PIPESTATUS[0]}"
New drbd meta data block successfully created.
0
$ rm -rf "$tmpdir" "$img"
